1
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:12,229
Every year, medical students who finish school need a place at a teaching hospital,

2
00:00:12,229 --> 00:00:13,876
which is called a residency.

3
00:00:13,876 --> 00:00:19,810
So on one side there are residency slots, and on the other side there are graduates.

4
00:00:19,983 --> 00:00:23,054
This is the matching problem of the last episode,

5
00:00:23,054 --> 00:00:27,881
with hospitals in the place of jobs and graduates in the place of candidates.

6
00:00:27,881 --> 00:00:33,737
Today a computer takes ranked lists from both sides and runs propose and reject on them.

7
00:00:33,737 --> 00:00:40,319
But the road there was long, and each failed attempt shows why the algorithm is built as it is.

8
00:00:42,483 --> 00:00:49,095
Residencies began about a century ago, and hospitals liked them, because an intern is cheap labour.

9
00:00:49,095 --> 00:00:53,259
Soon there were more slots than graduates to fill them.

10
00:00:53,416 --> 00:00:58,504
Now think like one of these hospitals, which does not want to be left with an empty slot.

11
00:00:58,504 --> 00:01:03,556
If it waits, the graduates it wants may already have said yes to another hospital.

12
00:01:03,556 --> 00:01:08,914
So the safe move is to make its offer a little earlier than everybody else.

13
00:01:09,083 --> 00:01:14,591
But every hospital thinks the same way, so the offers crept earlier year after year.

14
00:01:14,591 --> 00:01:19,419
By the middle of the nineteen forties they arrived at the beginning of the junior year,

15
00:01:19,419 --> 00:01:20,973
two years before graduation.

16
00:01:20,973 --> 00:01:25,635
Some hospitals were even thinking about asking sophomores.

17
00:01:25,816 --> 00:01:30,400
An offer that early is a bet on a student the hospital hardly knows yet.

18
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:33,110
So the American Medical Association stepped in,

19
00:01:33,110 --> 00:01:38,586
and told the schools to keep transcripts and reference letters locked up until the senior year.

20
00:01:38,586 --> 00:01:45,350
Without those papers an early offer had nothing to stand on, and the race to be first was over.

21
00:01:47,516 --> 00:01:51,776
But now every hospital was making its offers in the same short season.

22
00:01:51,776 --> 00:01:55,623
A hospital whose offer sat unanswered could lose its second and

23
00:01:55,623 --> 00:01:58,614
third choices to other hospitals in the meantime.

24
00:01:58,614 --> 00:02:02,167
So hospitals attached a deadline to every offer,

25
00:02:02,167 --> 00:02:06,830
and in the end a student had only a few hours to say yes or no.

26
00:02:06,983 --> 00:02:09,637
Now look at it from the side of the graduate.

27
00:02:09,637 --> 00:02:14,917
She has an offer in front of her, and a hospital she likes more has not answered yet.

28
00:02:14,917 --> 00:02:20,129
With a few hours on the clock she must take it or risk ending up with nothing.

29
00:02:20,283 --> 00:02:23,343
Compare that with the candidates in the last episode,

30
00:02:23,343 --> 00:02:27,673
who could answer maybe and keep an offer in hand while better ones arrived.

31
00:02:27,673 --> 00:02:30,979
The short deadline took exactly that answer away.

32
00:02:30,979 --> 00:02:35,997
So the missing piece was a way to hold an offer without closing the door.

33
00:02:37,950 --> 00:02:44,874
In the early nineteen fifties this led to one central system, called the National Residency Matching Program.

34
00:02:44,874 --> 00:02:53,016
Every hospital handed in a ranked list of graduates, and every graduate handed in a ranked list of hospitals.

35
00:02:53,750 --> 00:02:57,476
The program then paired everyone up from those lists.

36
00:02:57,476 --> 00:03:03,430
But at first its pairing could contain a hospital and a graduate who would both rather have each other.

37
00:03:03,430 --> 00:03:07,122
That is exactly the trouble we met in the last episode,

38
00:03:07,122 --> 00:03:11,284
and such a pair has every reason to make a private deal again.

39
00:03:11,450 --> 00:03:16,084
In nineteen fifty-two the program switched to propose and reject.

40
00:03:16,084 --> 00:03:18,833
A matching with no such pair is called stable,

41
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:23,734
and that is what the algorithm produced, so nobody had a reason to deal privately.

42
00:03:23,734 --> 00:03:31,460
The offers and the answers of maybe all happen inside the computer, and people only see the final result.

43
00:03:31,616 --> 00:03:33,984
Sixty years later, in twenty twelve,

44
00:03:33,984 --> 00:03:40,824
Lloyd Shapley and Alvin Roth received the Nobel Prize in economics for work that extends this algorithm.

45
00:03:40,824 --> 00:03:44,172
So the word that carried the whole story is stable.

46
00:03:44,172 --> 00:03:49,848
What exactly it demands of a matching is the question of the next episode.

